{"version":"ai-mcq-bank-v2-la-batch15-high-quality-rewritten-affected-2026-05-22-stem-fixed-inequality-rendering-fixed-student-facing-qc-2026-05-31-pt-foundations-2026-06-11-pt-batch2-foundations-counting-2026-06-11-pt-batch3-counting-probability-rules-2026-06-11-pt-batch4-conditioning-2026-06-11-pt-batch5-random-variables-2026-06-11-pt-batch6-discrete-distributions-2026-06-11-pt-batch7-distribution-foundations-2026-06-11-pt-batch8-continuous-expectation-2026-06-11-pt-batch9-non-week7-expectation-transform-2026-06-11-pt-batch10-non-week7-process-summary-2026-06-11-pt-batch11-non-week7-final-summary-shape-2026-06-11-quality-round1-2026-06-14-quality-round2-2026-06-14-quality-round3-2026-06-14-quality-round4-deep-2026-06-14+quality-round5-difficulty-2026-06-14+quality-round6-expression-2026-06-14+quality-round7-answer-cue-2026-06-14+quality-round8-answer-shape-2026-06-14+quality-round9-answer-position-2026-06-14+quality-round10-template-padding-2026-06-14+quality-round11-generic-stems-2026-06-14+quality-round12-deterministic-cleanup-2026-06-15+quality-pilot-deep-rework-2026-06-15","concept_id":"Year-1/1b-Math-II-Linear-Algebra/transpose.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/1b-Math-II-Linear-Algebra/transpose.html","questions":[{"id":"la_batch4_transpose_001","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For any real $m\\times n$ matrix $A$, which product is always defined and square?","options":["$A^{T}A$","$A+A^{T}$","$A^{2}$","$AA$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"85dfcfe20aa7d968eaf5c235"},{"id":"la_batch4_transpose_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is the entry formula for the transpose?","options":["$(A^T)_{ij}=0$ for all $i,j$.","$(A^T)_{ij}=a_{ij}+1$.","$(A^T)_{ij}=a_{ji}$.","$(A^T)_{ij}=a_{ij}a_{ji}$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0d94748259dea88ce796d19b"},{"id":"la_batch4_transpose_003","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If $A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&2&3\\\\4&5&6\\end{pmatrix}$, which matrix is $A^T$?","options":["$\\begin{pmatrix}1&4\\\\2&5\\\\3&6\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}1&2&3\\\\4&5&6\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}4&5&6\\\\1&2&3\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\\\3&4\\\\5&6\\end{pmatrix}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"011806d73bc026f5f1e87c03"},{"id":"la_batch4_transpose_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What happens if you transpose twice?","options":["You always get the identity matrix.","You always get the zero matrix.","You get back the original matrix.","You get the negative of the original matrix."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"89fced83b748ea072ce8984a"},{"id":"la_batch4_transpose_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"How does the transpose of a product behave?","options":["$(AB)^T=AB$ for all matrices","$(AB)^T=A^TB^T$ always.","$(AB)^T=B^TA^T$ when defined","$(AB)^T=A+B$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ca5fe548ca166eef01e7a175"},{"id":"la_batch4_transpose_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\\\3&4\\end{pmatrix}$, what is $A^T$?","options":["$\\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\\\3&4\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}1&3\\\\2&4\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}4&3\\\\2&1\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\\\4&3\\end{pmatrix}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f4795508331a9a454e180ac4"},{"id":"la_batch4_transpose_007","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What is the transpose of $\\begin{pmatrix}1&2&3\\end{pmatrix}$?","options":["$\\begin{pmatrix}3&2&1\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\2\\\\3\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}1&0&0\\\\0&2&0\\\\0&0&3\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}1&2&3\\end{pmatrix}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"28da99e7ce2305fe3bf83639"},{"id":"la_batch4_transpose_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"The sum $A+A^{T}$ is defined only when $A$ is:","options":["$2\\times2$","square","symmetric","invertible"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ecc15f77cac2ecdafde4dee9"},{"id":"la_batch4_transpose_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $A=\\begin{pmatrix}0&7&8\\\\1&2&3\\end{pmatrix}$, what is the $(3,1)$ entry of $A^T$?","options":["$3$","$8$","$1$","$7$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a778be2ef30c6493023c9877"},{"id":"la_batch4_transpose_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $A=\\begin{pmatrix}2\\\\-1\\\\4\\end{pmatrix}$, what is $A^T$?","options":["$\\begin{pmatrix}2\\\\-1\\\\4\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}4&-1&2\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}2&-1&4\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}-2&1&-4\\end{pmatrix}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"dad591b89d7ab7df732d0c43"},{"id":"la_batch4_transpose_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does the order reverse in $(AB)^T=B^TA^T$?","options":["Rows of $AB$ use $B$ then $A$, so transposing reverses order","There is no reversal; the correct formula is always $A^TB^T$","Transpose makes all products commutative.","The order reverses only when $A=B$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"92d1d906c6adc6484978b4f6"},{"id":"la_batch4_transpose_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does $(A^T)^T=A$ say conceptually?","options":["Transpose always leaves a matrix unchanged.","Transpose is defined only once for a matrix","Flipping rows and columns twice restores the original","Two transposes produce the zero matrix."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"18bcfaa669faf1a54834f499"},{"id":"la_batch4_transpose_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Can a non-square matrix be equal to its transpose?","options":["Yes, every matrix equals its transpose.","No, because $A$ and $A^T$ have different sizes unless the matrix is square.","No, because transposes are undefined for non-square matrices.","Yes, whenever the matrix has a zero entry."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e9359b862a7760226164fc79"},{"id":"la_batch4_transpose_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $A$ be $2\\times3$ and $B$ be $3\\times4$. What is the size of $(AB)^T$?","options":["$4\\times3$","$2\\times4$","$3\\times3$","$4\\times2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"14e496bb9bafd2af5050fcaa"},{"id":"la_batch4_transpose_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\\\3&4\\end{pmatrix}$, what is $(A^T)^T$?","options":["$\\begin{pmatrix}4&3\\\\2&1\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}1&3\\\\2&4\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\\\3&4\\end{pmatrix}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ab17aec6eefcb943ca80da8f"},{"id":"la_batch4_transpose_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $A$ is $2\\times3$ and $B$ is $3\\times2$, what is the correct expression for $(AB)^T$?","options":["$B^TA^T$","$A^TB$","$BA$","$A^TB^T$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4d8a83fb3bfad619f760e71b"},{"id":"la_batch4_transpose_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does transposition turn row vectors into column vectors?","options":["It multiplies the vector by the identity matrix.","It deletes all but the first entry.","It changes every entry into its reciprocal.","A $1\\times n$ matrix becomes an $n\\times1$ matrix."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1ac2d3b487fed7a41d70c5c4"},{"id":"la_batch4_transpose_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If $A^T=A$, what special kind of square matrix is being suggested?","options":["A permutation matrix necessarily.","A symmetric matrix.","A non-square matrix.","A zero matrix necessarily."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9631aa01e9fdd98d556b8121"},{"id":"la_batch4_transpose_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $A$ be $3\\times2$ and $B$ be $2\\times5$. Which product equals $(AB)^T$?","options":["$B^TA^T$, of size $5\\times3$","$A^TB$, of size $2\\times5$","$A^TB^T$, of size $2\\times2$","$B^TA$, of size $5\\times2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a95b134315d34cee3d0f6718"},{"id":"la_batch4_transpose_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"For $A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&2&0\\\\3&4&5\\end{pmatrix}$, which entry of $A$ becomes the $(1,2)$ entry of $A^T$?","options":["$a_{11}=1$","$a_{22}=4$","$a_{21}=3$","$a_{12}=2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c64736332745dd1a96091624"}]}}